// Original foundation capstone; reuse existing fictional labs and note storage.
const h=v=>({t:'h',v}),p=v=>({t:'p',v}),key=v=>({t:'key',v}),tip=v=>({t:'tip',v});
const term=(v,note)=>({t:'new',v,note}),check=(q,a,why)=>({q,a,why}),ref=(title,url)=>({title,url});
module.exports={
 '8.1':{
  objective:'Connect assets, threats, vulnerabilities and controls to one concrete task, then trace a login/read request across device, network, service and storage boundaries.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Finish with a task, not a list of tools'),
   p('You have learned devices and networks, identity and data protection, application boundaries, controls and evidence. This final week connects them. Maya’s fictional task is simple: read her private note and keep a recoverable copy. Explain which assets support that task, what could go wrong and what evidence would establish the required outcome. Installing a new tool is not the starting requirement.'),
   term('Asset, threat, vulnerability and control — connected review','An asset is something you value or depend on. A threat is a possible source/event of harm. A vulnerability is a weakness relevant to that harm. A control is a measure intended to change likelihood or consequence. An asset being valuable does not by itself prove it is vulnerable or compromised.'),
   p('Example chain: asset—Maya’s note and its access; threat—someone gaining unintended access; possible weakness—missing owner-note permission check; control—backend authorisation plus appropriate account protection; verification—an owner can read N1 while a non-owner is refused under the policy. Add recovery separately: preserving privacy does not automatically make yesterday’s note recoverable after loss.'),
   h('2. Explain the required security outcome'),
   term('Confidentiality, integrity and availability — task review','Confidentiality concerns the intended readers, integrity the intended state/change rules, and availability useful access when needed. These requirements can apply differently to public lessons, private notes, credentials and recovery copies.'),
   p('The public teaching page is intentionally readable by visitors. A private note has a different reader requirement. The note must retain its permitted edits and remain usable; a backup also needs appropriate readers and a verified recovery path. Do not infer that making everything public, encrypting everything or blocking every connection is a complete solution to all three requirements.'),
   h('3. Trace the device and network part'),
   term('Dependency and trust boundary — trace review','A dependency is something the task relies on, such as device software, a network path or an identity service. A trust boundary marks a change in control/assumptions where the receiving component must enforce the appropriate checks.'),
   p('For the fictional online note service: browser/device → local network → provider/internet path → named service. DNS helps locate the intended endpoint; a working local Wi-Fi connection does not prove the external service is available. TLS protects communication with a verified named endpoint under its assumptions, not the honesty of every page or the privacy of data intentionally delivered to visitors.'),
   p('The same person may open the offline Study Hub HTML without this online journey. Local notes use that browser’s storage context unless a configured feature shares/syncs data. Keep those two architectures separate in your explanation: a backend/API in the fictional note-service case is not an unnoticed new backend added to Study Hub.'),
   h('4. Trace account, request and stored data'),
   p('Known login route → accepted authenticator → established session → read-note request → session validation → resource/action permission → intended data access → appropriate output. A later edit also needs its input contract and suitable intent/CSRF defence for the credential model, then safe data handling and truthful persistence status. A200 response still needs the expected note rather than an unrelated login page.'),
   p('A valid session cannot replace ownership checks. A correctly formatted ID cannot establish permission. SQL binding and output handling enforce different interpretation boundaries. A firewall path cannot enforce every application rule. State the relevant evidence for each step instead of awarding the whole journey a green tick because one layer worked.'),
   h('5. Draw and test one integrated story'),
   p('Paper exercise: label browser, account/session service, application and fictional note store. Draw the permitted read/edit paths and one denied non-owner path. For each arrow, write purpose, controlled data, required check and evidence limitation. Use Week5’s offline request lab to check owner/guest/non-owner model outcomes, while stating that its public selectors are not real authentication and no actual HTTP server is contacted.'),
   p('Worked conclusion: Maya’s model owner-read succeeds; Ravi’s model N1 read is denied; those demonstrate the simulated policy, not an audit of an actual hosted account. Next evidence for a real permitted deployment would include its real session/authorisation implementation and scoped tests. Learning to state this limit is part of the foundation, not a reason to stop practising.'),
   key('Trace one useful task through assets, dependencies, account permission, interpretation and recovery. Name the requirement and evidence at each boundary; avoid transferring one control’s success to unrelated layers.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Does a public lesson page need the same reader policy as a private journal?','No. Their intended audiences and data requirements differ.','Set the requirement from the asset and purpose rather than one blanket sharing rule.'),
   check('A firewall permits the browser to reach a service. Are owner-note permissions established?','No. The application must still decide the caller’s resource/action access.','Network paths and application permission cover different boundaries.'),
   check('What does a200 response need before you call note retrieval successful?','The expected permitted note representation/data, under the request’s actual context.','A delivered login page or error message can have a status that does not establish the intended business result.'),
   check('What does Week5’s offline lab establish when an owner-read succeeds?','The fictional in-memory model’s result, not a real server/account security audit.','Transfer the reasoning carefully and obtain appropriate implementation evidence for a real authorised service.')
  ],
  reflection:'Draw the login/read/edit task map. Link one asset, threat, relevant weakness, control and verification to each important boundary. Include one offline-versus-online architecture distinction and one recovery requirement.',
  references:[ref('NIST: risk-management outcomes','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework'),ref('OWASP: resource/action authorisation','https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html'),ref('Week5 offline request model','docs/cybersecurity-week5/request-lab.html')]
 },
 '8.2':{
  objective:'Review account/update/permission/recovery requirements with bounded evidence, then organise related actions under the six NIST CSF2.0 functions without treating them as a certification checklist.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Review ordinary protection before adding complexity'),
   p('Use a fictional device/account set, or read only information you are permitted to observe in your own learning environment. The lesson does not require real credential values, sensitive screenshots or account changes. An ordinary review asks which accounts/resources matter, who owns maintenance, what update/permission evidence exists, and whether recovery has actually worked.'),
   term('Protection review row','A row connecting the intended requirement, the observed evidence/time, match/mismatch/unknown, owner and next step. Unknown is a useful status, not an empty cell to fill with assumed compliance.'),
   p('Example rows: unique account secrets/MFA—status documented without the secrets; update activation—verified active version or pending/unknown; ordinary user privileges—observed role relevant to the task; private resource access—reader policy checked; recovery—latest usable point and test result. A completed-looking form is not adequate if every observation was copied from the desired baseline.'),
   h('2. Reconnect account and permission dependencies'),
   p('Protect the account that resets other accounts and the manager/recovery routes that support them. Keep real secrets outside study notes. A valid passkey does not settle every fallback/session question; a standard-user context does not mean personal data is unreadable. For a workload integration, understand the supported credential scope and lifetime rather than grant all access for convenience.'),
   p('A public static website serves its shipped content to visitors; local browser storage and configured sync have separate reader boundaries. An unlisted/secret link is not the same as authenticated private readership. Repository visibility or a masked field cannot recall a credential already exposed elsewhere. These are decisions about actual access paths, not just the label shown by an interface.'),
   h('3. Use the six functions as an organising map'),
   term('NIST CSF2.0 functions','Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover organise cybersecurity outcomes. They interact and can operate concurrently. They are not a mandatory six-step incident sequence, an exhaustive action checklist or a course-issued security certification.'),
   p('Govern: assign risk/decision roles and policies. Identify: understand important assets, dependencies and concerns. Protect: apply suitable safeguards. Detect: notice relevant evidence with healthy coverage. Respond: coordinate assessed incident actions. Recover: restore useful operations and communicate recovery. This is an original plain-English orientation; consult the official framework for detailed outcomes.'),
   h('4. Map one task across several functions'),
   term('Outcome mapping','Linking a task/evidence item to a relevant outcome, with a reason. One task can support several functions, and a mapped label does not prove the outcome was achieved.'),
   p('Fictional backup task: Govern names the owner and tolerated loss; Identify records critical files and recovery dependencies; Protect limits readers and preserves copies; Detect notices failed backup collection; Respond coordinates harm reduction if an incident affects them; Recover verifies useful restore. Do not count six labels as six successful tests. Evidence for the restore point and actual data usability still matters.'),
   p('Another task—updating an exposed component—connects inventory/priority, a protected change, observation and possible response. A supported update announcement is not active-state verification. A temporary path restriction can reduce exposure without removing the weakness. Write the remaining question and owner instead of marking the whole framework complete.'),
   h('5. Finish a small review with evidence'),
   p('Worked row: recovery target is at most one day of lost notes; latest usable copy is three days old. Result: mismatch, source current recovery test, owner designated learner/lab administrator, next step repair and retest the schedule/recovery chain. Function labels help organise that concern but do not change the failed target. A second row with an unavailable permission snapshot is unknown, not a confirmed breach.'),
   p('Create five review rows and map them with brief reasons. Include at least one observed match, one mismatch and one unknown in the fictional set. A real setting change needs its own justified authority and recovery plan; this learning task is to make the reasoning clear. Document review triggers such as failed tests, changed services or new authoritative guidance.'),
   key('Personal protection depends on accountable requirements and current evidence. The six-function map organises outcomes; it does not turn labels or intentions into demonstrated security.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Are the six functions a compulsory linear sequence for every incident?','No. They organise interacting outcomes, including concurrent activities.','An organising framework is not a universal step-by-step response script.'),
   check('A row says MFA enabled but was copied from a desired policy. Is it verified evidence?','No. It states an expectation rather than an observed current condition.','Separate required state and observed state, keeping unknowns explicit.'),
   check('A backup task maps to all six functions. Does that prove a usable restore?','No. The relevant restore/data/dependency checks still need actual evidence.','Mapping explains relevance, not achievement.'),
   check('What should a review record instead of passwords or token values?','Protection status, source/time, role/dependency, uncertainty and accountable next step.','A review can describe security without creating another credential copy.')
  ],
  reflection:'Create five fictional personal-security review rows and map them to the six functions with reasons. Include a failed recovery target and an unknown permission observation; state the responsible next steps.',
  references:[ref('NIST: CSF2.0 and framework limits','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework/faqs'),ref('NCSC: recovery-capable backups','https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online/always-back-up-your-most-important-data')]
 },
 '8.3':{
  objective:'Reconcile the prepared network/endpoint/auth/application records, preserve time/identity uncertainty, and write a supported multi-source conclusion with clear next evidence questions.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Reuse the evidence rather than invent new incidents'),
   p('Open or revisit Week7’s prepared fictional records. You have12 deliveries,11 distinct source events and one undocumented event timezone. The exercise is to connect evidence, not collect your real logs or add a dramatic attack story. Record the collector cutoff00:08:30 UTC and the stated assumption that offset-bearing source clocks are correct for this worksheet only.'),
   term('Cross-source correlation','Connecting records through documented meaning and compatible context such as a non-credential reference, asset/action and time. Similar IP/account strings alone do not establish the same person or complete causal chain.'),
   p('Keep four source questions visible: authentication asks which account decision was recorded; application asks which protected task/result was reported; network asks about the communication path; endpoint asks what the host/tool observed. A source can help another question, but should not be forced to supply a claim outside its coverage.'),
   h('2. Reconcile identities and chronology first'),
   p('L03/L04 are two deliveries of auth eventA03. Late L12 is eventA04 at00:01:30 UTC, despite arriving00:08:30. X01 lacks a source timezone, so its UTC event position stays unknown. Known-time order remains A01,A02,A04,A03,A10,N01,P01,P02,E01,Z01. A sorted view must retain raw references and unknown rows; it is not a replacement for collected evidence.'),
   term('Data cutoff and comparison unit','The cutoff describes which records were available to a view. The unit describes what is counted—deliveries, distinct source events, failures or alerts. Different cutoffs/units can produce valid but different numbers.'),
   p('The alert at00:07 counted A01/A02/A03 because A04 had not arrived. The final worksheet has four unique failures in [00:00,00:03) UTC. Z01 is not a fifth failure. Report those assumptions instead of treating the earlier count as a false log or silently averaging counts from different views.'),
   h('3. What the connected records actually support'),
   p('A10 reports accepted account access for Maya. C-7, an explicitly fictional non-secret reference, links that context to P01 and P02. P01 reports refused editing of Ravi’s N2 and no write. P02 reports a persisted edit of the account’s own N1. N01 shows an allowed connection to APP-A on443 from the same documentation address used in A10, but this similarity does not independently identify a human or link every network action to the account.'),
   p('E01 is an agent heartbeat on LAB-A. It supports that specific received health observation, not complete endpoint coverage or a clean malware verdict. A client success message, a backend persistence log and independently verified stored state are different evidence levels. State which level the case supplies instead of turning one source claim into universal certainty.'),
   h('4. Make alternatives testable'),
   term('Alternative explanation and discriminating evidence','An alternative explanation is another plausible account of the observations. Discriminating evidence is information that can distinguish explanations. Listing possibilities without a next evidence question is incomplete investigation.'),
   p('AlternativeA: a permitted job had stale credentials, followed by normal user activity. AlternativeB: some account access was misused. Missing information includes actual job/source/credential history, trusted user confirmation and relevant note state/history. Both need authorised evidence; neither is established by a plausible schedule or an accepted account label.'),
   p('Do not call the refused N2 operation a successful private-data write or claim exfiltration from a network allow record. An IP can be shared/translated, and a control can block one operation while other account use still deserves context. The task is a bounded conclusion with questions, not a verdict forced from an incomplete worksheet.'),
   h('5. Write a connected-evidence summary'),
   p('Model summary: at the stated cutoff the prepared auth source reports four distinct failures and one accepted account event; related application records report one refused non-owner edit and one persisted owner-note edit. Network and endpoint records add limited path/health context. Human attribution, intended use, independent stored state and X01’s event timezone remain unresolved. Request job/user/state context through the designated owner. No live containment was performed in this exercise.'),
   p('Use this summary as a pattern, not a phrase to copy without checking facts. In your own version, cite at least four raw references, give a distinct-event count, one uncertainty and one evidence request. Later automation can organise these fields, but the inference limits should remain visible in the report.'),
   key('Connect sources only within their meaning, identity and time limits. Correct counts and a tidy timeline support investigation; they do not independently establish cause, person or every task outcome.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Does N01’s allowed connection establish a successful unauthorised note change?','No. It reports a communication outcome, not the complete protected application action.','Use relevant application/state evidence and preserve that source’s limitations.'),
   check('What is C-7 allowed to support in this exercise?','The documented link among the prepared account/application observations, not a human identity or usable credential.','A correlation label supplies a relationship under its stated meaning only.'),
   check('Why should X01 remain in the evidence package?','It records an actual prepared delivery and a source-time uncertainty, even though it cannot enter the known UTC event order.','Unknown evidence should be preserved and labelled rather than deleted to make a cleaner story.'),
   check('What makes two alternative explanations useful?','A permitted next question or evidence item that could distinguish them.','Plausibility alone cannot choose a cause; state what would change the conclusion.')
  ],
  reflection:'Write a connected summary using four raw references, the final cutoff and the correct failure count. Include source-specific limits, two hypotheses and a discriminating evidence request, without assigning a real person or confirmed attack.',
  references:[ref('Week7 prepared source records','docs/cybersecurity-week7/sample-events.json'),ref('Week7 assumptions and case exercises','docs/cybersecurity-week7/case-notes.txt'),ref('OWASP: source context and protected logs','https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html')]
 },
 '8.4':{
  objective:'Prepare a small scoped learning-lab assessment, distinguish review evidence from a real-service test, and write findings with ownership, uncertainty and verification criteria.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Define the authorised learning task'),
   term('Assessment scope and rules of engagement','Scope identifies the permitted assets, actions, time/data boundaries and responsible people. Rules of engagement specify constraints, contacts, stopping conditions and handling of results. Owning one laptop does not authorise testing every external system it contacts.'),
   p('Choose the fictional course lab, or a clearly authorised read-only review of your own learning setup. For this beginner assessment, exclude probing third-party services, changing production settings, accessing other users’ data or collecting credentials. Review allowed records/settings and the existing simulation. A later actual technical test needs its own defined permission and method; writing an assessment does not grant it automatically.'),
   p('Fictional scope statement: assess the course’s offline request model and prepared evidence package for specified learning requirements; do not claim they implement a real authenticated backend; no network targets; no live data; baseline records retained. A personal review can use privately kept observations such as update status and a fictional restore test, but publish only a sanitised learning version.'),
   h('2. Inventory dependencies and required outcomes'),
   term('Assessment evidence plan','A plan linking each requirement to a permitted observation, expected result, source/time and limitation. It says how a conclusion will be supported, not just which tool might be used.'),
   p('Rows might include: main course loads offline; notes can be saved/restored in a fictional test profile; owner/non-owner request-model decisions follow the model policy; reference handout bytes match the teaching manifest; known-offset timelines preserve unknowns. Real device support or account protection can be reviewed separately with appropriate current evidence. Keep simulated findings distinct from actual observations.'),
   h('3. Perform bounded checks and keep unavailable results'),
   p('The Week5 lab provides owner/guest/non-owner and invalid-input cases. It displays model decisions and in-memory state; it does not validate real sessions or execute real SQL. The Week7 digest compares exact teaching-file bytes; it does not verify truth or legal sufficiency. An unavailable handout should be recorded as unavailable, not changed into a guessed hash match.'),
   term('Assessment result and evidence gap','A result states whether the specified observed check met its requirement. An evidence gap names what could not be established. A gap is not automatically a security flaw, but it limits the claim you can make.'),
   p('For a real own-lab read-only review, a pending required update can be a confirmed maintenance gap; a version you cannot verify is unknown applicability, not a confirmed vulnerability. A permission screenshot may document configuration but still leave behaviour untested. Keep source severity separate from local priority, and do not invent a CVE for a baseline difference.'),
   h('4. Write a useful finding and next step'),
   term('Finding/retest contract — capstone review','A finding connects asset, requirement, observed state, evidence confidence, consequence, owner/action and closure test. Remediation, temporary mitigation and accepted risk are different statuses with different evidence.'),
   p('Worked fictional finding F-01: no recorded restore test for a sample learning archive. Requirement: usable recovery under stated loss/time targets. Evidence: archive exists, but no restore result is supplied. Conclusion: recovery not demonstrated, not confirmed archive corruption. Owner: learner/lab administrator. Action: test invented records in a separate destination, retain the source and measure useful recovery. Closure: expected records and representative read/write work with required keys/access.'),
   p('Worked F-02: active update state unknown after a download. Request the responsible owner’s current build/restart evidence and applicable authoritative guidance. Do not run an exploit to force a verdict or apply a broad system change to make the worksheet complete. The report should remain useful even when the next evidence item has not yet been obtained.'),
   h('5. Connect Python automation to the evidence pipeline'),
   term('Automation boundary','The permitted inputs, operations and outputs of an automation task. A script inherits its own access context; its ability to read or contact something is not the same as permission to assess it.'),
   p('Python can later connect inventory comparison, integrity checks and prepared log timelines into one consistent report. Keep source asset/event identities, original timestamps, digest representation and unknowns. It should not merge different assets by display name, average unrelated severity scores, infer missing timezones, print candidate secret values or silently repair settings. Use fictional fixtures to test these requirements before a scoped real use.'),
   p('Your report deliverable names scope, diagram/inventory, evidence plan, results/gaps, two findings and verification steps. An optional assessment workbook and worked example are linked below. They are plain-text handouts; essential instructions remain here for offline learning. The example contains only invented conditions and is not an audit of your website or devices.'),
   key('A small useful assessment is authorised, bounded and evidence-backed. Separate simulation, observed fact and uncertainty; make findings actionable through an owner and observable retest.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('You own the laptop. Does that automatically authorise assessing every hosted service it uses?','No. External systems and data require their own permission and scope.','Ownership of the client does not transfer authority across all dependencies.'),
   check('The archive exists, but no restore was attempted. Is it confirmed corrupt?','No. Recovery is not demonstrated; corruption is a different claim requiring evidence.','Preserve the exact evidence gap instead of replacing uncertainty with a dramatic finding.'),
   check('What should a finding include after its observed gap?','Relevant consequence/confidence, owner/action and a concrete closure test.','A useful report must support an accountable next step and evidence of completion.'),
   check('May an automation silently fill unknown timezones or apply fixes to finish its report?','No. Preserve unknowns and keep operations within the approved automation boundary.','Convenient output is not permission to invent evidence or change the system.')
  ],
  reflection:'Complete the assessment workbook for the fictional course lab, or a privately documented permitted read-only own-lab review. Produce two findings with clear evidence gaps and retests. Publish only sanitised fictional material.',
  references:[ref('NIST: planning scoped security assessment','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/115/final'),ref('NIST: outcome and evidence context','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework'),ref('Assessment workbook — plain-text template','docs/cybersecurity-week8/assessment-workbook.txt'),ref('Worked fictional assessment','docs/cybersecurity-week8/worked-example.txt')]
 },
 '8.5':{
  objective:'Prepare an owner-led response/recovery checklist and a small learning portfolio that preserves source references, protects sensitive data and defines usable recovery evidence.',
  sections:[
   h('1. The checklist starts before an incident'),
   term('Response/recovery plan — consolidated review','A plan names who assesses, decides, communicates and performs actions; the permitted scope; needed evidence; containment trade-offs; recovery dependencies and verification. It should be usable when some usual account/device is unavailable.'),
   p('For a fictional class service, name responsible roles rather than inventing real contact details: learner reports an observation, administrator gathers authorised system evidence, designated lead decides incident/containment steps, and service owner approves restoration. In a real environment, actual contact routes belong in a protected plan, not public course notes.'),
   h('2. Make each decision measurable'),
   p('Checklist stages: record observation/cutoff and scope; preserve appropriate raw evidence; separate facts/hypotheses/gaps; assess urgency and decision authority; choose proportionate containment; address identified causes; restore intended data/service; verify functions/protections/collection health; communicate supported status; assign improvements and retests. Activities can overlap, and urgent harm reduction may precede perfect evidence collection under the authorised lead’s plan.'),
   term('Decision point and stopping condition','A decision point states what evidence/authority is needed before an action. A stopping condition identifies when the learner or test must pause/escalate, such as unexpected live personal data, operational harm or a target outside the permission boundary.'),
   p('Do not automatically delete logs, reboot devices or replace browser data to make a warning disappear. Do not keep a harmful access route active solely because a classroom checklist says preserve everything first. Explain the trade-off and responsible decision. Requested, approved and performed containment must be labelled separately.'),
   h('3. Verify useful recovery, not just a filename'),
   p('Use the stated RPO/RTO and current usable recovery-point evidence. Check needed access/keys, software/import compatibility, expected records and representative permitted read/write tasks. A matching archive digest supports exact bytes, not full usability. A three-day-old usable copy misses a one-day loss target even if the backup schedule says daily.'),
   term('Recovery evidence record','A record naming the recovery source/capture point, destination, responsible role, access/key dependencies, task results, elapsed recovery time and limitations. Keep the original and test in a separate permitted destination when the plan allows it.'),
   p('Fictional practice: restore two invented notes into a separate test browser/profile, check their expected contents and practise another save. Do not overwrite actual Study Hub progress as part of the classroom exercise. If only a worksheet was completed, report planned test, not tested recovery. If a source was unavailable, record the gap instead of marking pass.'),
   h('4. Collect a portfolio that shows reasoning'),
   term('Learning portfolio','A small collection of your own explanations, diagrams, fictional evidence analyses and reports. It demonstrates what you can explain and verify, not a guarantee that an employer, certification or real service accepts a security claim.'),
   p('Recommended contents for this foundation: a glossary with your own examples; a device/network/request diagram with boundaries; account/data-reader and responsibility maps; an input/access test matrix; a baseline/finding report; the Week7 timeline/case summary; a response/recovery checklist; and a short reflection naming uncertainty and improvements. Keep each artefact’s scope, source/cutoff and model-versus-real limitation.'),
   p('The glossary should explain relationships: an alert is not an incident verdict, authentication is not resource permission, a hash is not encryption, exposure is not proof of exploitation, and cloud/shared responsibility does not mean unassigned work. Include one counterexample for each difficult pair so you can use the terms in a new situation.'),
   h('5. Protect and review the portfolio itself'),
   term('Redaction and private originals','Redaction removes sensitive material from a shared derivative while preserving needed meaning. Store any authorised originals under appropriate access/retention; a public summary must not contain credentials, private backup bodies or unnecessary personal identifiers.'),
   p('Use invented accounts and documentation addresses in public learning artefacts. A role label and evidence category can often explain the case without actual token values or personal paths. A hidden field, private-looking filename or unlisted link is not a substitute for reader policy. Verify every intended shared file rather than assume a clean source file makes exported copies safe.'),
   p('End with an after-action improvement row: issue, evidence, owner, change, review trigger and verification. For example, document an unknown source timezone and test the next prepared export; repair a recovery gap and demonstrate the required tasks. A promised improvement and a deployed/tested improvement have different statuses.'),
   key('A response/recovery checklist assigns decisions and verifies useful restoration. A good portfolio contains bounded, sanitised evidence and your own reasoning, with honest tested-versus-planned status.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Is a completed recovery worksheet identical to a successful restore test?','No. Label planned and tested work separately.','A written plan does not establish that access, keys, records and functions actually worked.'),
   check('Should a beginner’s checklist prescribe unconditional log deletion or reboot after an alert?','No. Actions depend on urgency, evidence, impact and authorised decision-making.','A blanket shortcut can harm operations or evidence without addressing the real concern.'),
   check('What belongs in a public learning portfolio?','Your own fictional/sanitised reasoning with scope, references and limitations, not real secrets or private incident bodies.','Demonstrate understanding without creating an unnecessary sensitive-data copy.'),
   check('What distinguishes an improvement promise from a completed improvement?','Evidence of the owned change and its required verification.','Statuses should describe what happened, not only the intended next step.')
  ],
  reflection:'Write the fictional response/recovery checklist and assemble the portfolio outline. Identify each artefact’s source and reader boundary. Include an untested item honestly and define how it would become verified.',
  references:[ref('NIST: coordinated response and recovery','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final'),ref('NCSC: usable recovery planning','https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online/always-back-up-your-most-important-data'),ref('Capstone workbook and portfolio prompts','docs/cybersecurity-week8/assessment-workbook.txt')]
 },
 '8.6':{
  objective:'Assess foundation readiness through explanation and fictional evidence tasks, choose a targeted revision path, and define a small safe automation/learning goal without equating course completion with expert competence.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Evaluate what you can do with a new example'),
   term('Readiness and transfer','Readiness means you can explain core relationships and apply them within an appropriate scope. Transfer means using an idea on a different example, not merely repeating the worked answer. A completed lesson date alone cannot demonstrate either.'),
   p('Use three labels for each skill: can explain, can demonstrate with fictional/approved evidence, or needs another attempt. Keep the example/reference that supports your label. Avoid an invented percentage that implies a universal professional standard. These eight weeks supply a foundation; continued supervised practice and deeper subject study are separate learning goals.'),
   h('2. Attempt the core tasks without notes'),
   p('TaskA: map one asset/threat/weakness/control and describe the remaining evidence gap. TaskB: trace a request across device/network/account/application/storage boundaries. TaskC: explain a permission matrix and an input/output contract. TaskD: prioritise a confirmed and an uncertain finding without treating CVSS as probability. TaskE: reconcile the Week7 event/receipt timeline. TaskF: define recovery targets and demonstrate or honestly plan a fictional restore. Each task needs a reason, not just a vocabulary label.'),
   p('Change one fact in each example: public lesson becomes private journal; network works but account permission fails;200 contains a login page; active version is unknown; event timezone is undocumented; backup point is too old. If your answer stays the same despite that change, identify which boundary you may have skipped.'),
   h('3. Choose revision by the actual gap'),
   term('Targeted revision','A plan linking a failed/unclear task to a specific earlier lesson, a fresh practice case and a recheck. It avoids rereading everything without knowing what needs improvement.'),
   p('If device/network roles are unclear, revisit Weeks1–3 and redraw a permitted request path. If account/readership/encoding boundaries are unclear, revisit Week4. If the backend/control sequence is unclear, revisit Week5 and the offline model. If severity/priority/maintenance differ poorly in your explanation, revisit Week6. If counts/times/incident certainty are unclear, revisit Week7’s prepared records. Take another attempt with a changed fictional case.'),
   h('4. Choose the next learning emphasis from your evidence'),
   p('Possible emphases are operating-system/network fundamentals with approved observation, defensive application checks in a personally owned isolated training service, or monitoring/evidence reasoning with prepared data. State the goal and permission boundary before choosing tools. Do not jump to probing a public target because you can name a vulnerability. You can deepen a weak area without buying a subscription or adding an unrelated complex setup.'),
   term('Small next-stage goal','A bounded outcome with prerequisites, permitted data/system, expected evidence and review criterion. It is more useful than learn all cybersecurity or run every tool.'),
   p('Example goal: produce a repeatable report from approved fictional inventory and logs, preserving unknowns, event identities and evidence references. Prerequisites: file/structured-data handling, aware time, grouping and basic tests in the Python path. Evidence: known fixture counts/order match expectations; unreadable/missing fields are flagged; raw inputs remain unchanged. Human decisions about priority and risk acceptance stay outside automatic guessing.'),
   h('5. Design helpful automation with limits'),
   term('Repeatable security-work automation','Automation performs the same permitted processing under explicit definitions and checks. It can reduce manual transcription but can also consistently repeat a bad assumption; test definitions and failure handling, not only the happy path.'),
   p('Connect inventory comparison, exact-byte integrity checks and event timelines through stable asset/file/event identities. Retain input source/time and output limitations. Do not average unrelated vulnerability scores, merge assets by display name, normalise unknown offsets as UTC or print candidate secrets. A failed parse/read belongs in a report state, not a silently omitted row that makes coverage look complete.'),
   p('Python supports organising approved records and producing consistent findings summaries. This lesson remains cybersecurity-first; it does not add a coding assignment to replace your Python course. The workbook asks you to write an input/output contract and two failure-case tests in plain English, then identify which existing Python lessons you would revise before implementing it later.'),
   h('6. Finish with an honest learning decision'),
   p('Record two strong tasks, two weak tasks, their supporting evidence and one next-stage goal. Choose a revision/recheck date or trigger suitable for you rather than rushing to a new title. If a result came only from a model, label it model evidence. If a task was not demonstrated, keep it needs another attempt. Your next learning choice should follow what you can explain and verify.'),
   key('Readiness is supported by explanation, transfer and scoped evidence. Revise the specific weak boundary, then choose a small next goal with testable definitions and honest limitations.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Does finishing all lesson pages alone demonstrate professional expertise?','No. Use explanation, changed examples and appropriate practical evidence to assess actual readiness.','Lesson completion is a learning record, not universal proof of independent competence.'),
   check('An undocumented timestamp was silently treated as UTC to finish a report. Is the automation correct?','No. Preserve the uncertainty and require source context before assigning the instant.','Repeatability cannot repair an unsupported assumption.'),
   check('How should a revision plan address a weak evidence-timeline task?','Revisit the specific time/identity lessons, attempt a changed fictional case and verify its expected result.','Targeted rechecks are more informative than repeatedly reading every week without a goal.'),
   check('What makes a next-stage goal useful?','Defined outcome, prerequisites, permitted scope/data, expected evidence and failure/review criteria.','A bounded task can be evaluated; an unrestricted tool list cannot establish the intended learning outcome.')
  ],
  reflection:'Complete the six readiness tasks and keep evidence for each label. Choose two targeted revisions and one small next-stage goal. Write a plain-English automation contract with an unknown-time and unreadable-input test.',
  references:[ref('NIST: outcomes and continuous improvement','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework'),ref('NIST: scoped assessment planning','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/115/final'),ref('Readiness and portfolio workbook','docs/cybersecurity-week8/assessment-workbook.txt')]
 },
 '8.7':{
  objective:'Complete a closed-notes foundation review with twenty reasoned checks, assemble the capstone assessment/portfolio and state remaining gaps before choosing a further scoped learning stage.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Review the whole foundation before adding more topics'),
   p('This is the final day of the eight-week foundation roadmap. You have material for56 study days; that is a curriculum boundary, not a claim that every learner has mastered every subject. Attempt the twenty checks below without notes. Give both an answer and a reason before revealing the model, then revisit the specific earlier lesson behind each unclear point.'),
   term('Foundation capstone','An integrated learning task connecting previously studied requirements, boundaries, evidence and accountable actions. It should demonstrate reasoning on a bounded case rather than introduce unplanned testing of real systems.'),
   h('2. Assemble one complete fictional assessment'),
   p('Your capstone includes: scope and permitted methods; an asset/dependency/request diagram; account/data-reader and six-function maps; owner/non-owner and input-contract tests in the offline model; a connected Week7 evidence summary; two findings with uncertainty/priority/owners/retests; and a response/recovery plan. Use the workbook and compare the worked fictional example only after your own attempt.'),
   p('The worked example reports recovery as not demonstrated where no restore occurred and update state as unknown where active evidence was unavailable. It does not invent an attack, CVE, successful account takeover or completed containment. A report that preserves limitations can be stronger than an impressive-looking report with invented certainty.'),
   h('3. Check your conclusions against the actual units'),
   p('For the reused log case:12 deliveries,11 source events,10 offset-bearing events, one unresolved event timezone and four unique failures in the stated UTC window. The earlier alert saw three because the fourth arrived late. An accepted account label and C-7 reference do not prove a human actor. Application refusal/persistence logs do not independently supply every state snapshot. Ensure your summary and diagram agree with those facts.'),
   term('Claim/evidence consistency','The statement must not exceed the supplied evidence, and related artefacts should use compatible scope, identity, time basis and cutoffs. A correct number in one table does not excuse a contradictory narrative elsewhere.'),
   p('Likewise, show that model denied writes leave model records unchanged, and label it simulation. Do not claim a real database query was verified by a browser-only lookup. A byte digest reference checks the teaching file, not source-event truth. The network/endpoint observations remain limited to their source meanings.'),
   h('4. Reconnect protection and recovery'),
   p('Your reader boundaries must distinguish authored public material, private notes, live credentials and recovery copies. A hidden field or public JS file is not a secret store; an unlisted link is not authenticated private access. Exposed credentials need the provider’s access response, not only newest-file cleanup. Update activation, evidence gaps, temporary mitigations and verified fixes have different statuses.'),
   p('Your response/recovery plan must name roles, urgency/evidence trade-offs, expected operations, loss/time targets and verified or planned state. A backup file, framework label, quiet dashboard or installed agent cannot alone establish protected useful recovery. Record which evidence would make a remaining task verified, and who may obtain it.'),
   h('5. Complete and sanitise the portfolio'),
   p('Collect your own glossary, diagrams, assessment, test matrix, timeline, findings and recovery/readiness notes. Keep raw references and scope/cutoff information where relevant. Use fictional or properly sanitised shared content. Do not publish passwords, token values, actual private backup bodies or unnecessary identifiers to prove you completed a lesson. Preserve any authorised sensitive originals privately under the relevant policy.'),
   p('End with two demonstrated skills, two unresolved skills, a specific revision path and one small further-learning goal. Further weeks or a new specialist path should be added only after choosing that goal; this update finishes the requested two-month foundation rather than silently expanding the roadmap. Use the existing All weeks button to revisit any day, and rest after the closed-notes review.'),
   key('A complete foundation portfolio explains useful tasks and protection boundaries with bounded evidence, accountable actions and honest recovery/readiness status.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('How do asset, threat, vulnerability and control connect?','Identify the valued task/data, possible harm, relevant weakness and suitable measure, then define evidence for its outcome.','A list of terms without their relationship cannot guide an assessment.'),
   check('Does a working Wi-Fi link prove an external learning service is available?','No. Local connectivity and the external dependency are different observations.','Trace which path and task failed instead of collapsing every layer into one internet status.'),
   check('Does successful authentication grant access to every private note?','No. Resource/action authorisation must still permit the particular operation.','Identity evidence and permission protect different requirements.'),
   check('Can a long password from a famous public quotation be assumed unpredictable?','No. Length does not remove predictable selection or publication.','Use an appropriate unique secret and protected authentication/recovery dependencies.'),
   check('An unexpected message asks for an OTP. What is the next verification route?','Do not supply it; independently use the known service/contact route to verify the claimed task.','Urgency, logos and polished writing are not proof of legitimate authority.'),
   check('What is the distinction among encoding, encryption and hashing?','Representation change, protected recoverable data using keys, and a digest comparison under defined assumptions.','Select the operation from the goal; unfamiliar-looking output is not automatically confidential.'),
   check('A page has HTTPS. Are its claims automatically honest and its shipped secrets private?','No. Connection protection does not establish content honesty or conceal public delivered code/data.','Confirm intended endpoint and reader/storage boundaries separately.'),
   check('Can frontend input validation replace backend shared-data checks?','No. The receiver must enforce trusted identity, permission and its input contract.','Client convenience is not the protected server-data boundary.'),
   check('What separates SQL values from instruction structure?','The driver’s supported parameter-binding interface with fixed/approved query structure.','Placeholders visible in a string alone do not prove data was bound safely.'),
   check('How do XSS and CSRF concerns differ?','Unintended browser interpretation versus unintended automatically authenticated state-changing requests.','They cross different boundaries and may both require controls in one application.'),
   check('Does a firewall-enabled label prove every required permission/control outcome?','No. Check applicable rules and behaviours, plus independent application controls.','One host/network observation cannot verify unrelated layers.'),
   check('Is CVSS severity a direct probability of compromise on your asset?','No. Local applicability, exposure, context and evidence still need assessment.','Severity and local risk/priority are separate fields.'),
   check('Is every cloud maintenance task owned by the provider?','No. The service-specific shared-responsibility split identifies the actual task owners.','Managed infrastructure does not remove customer identity/data/application responsibilities.'),
   check('Why can12 prepared log deliveries describe11 events and only4 failed logins?','One event is delivered twice, and other source events/alerts are different activity units.','Count documented identities and the question’s unit rather than every row as another failure.'),
   check('Can an undocumented local event clock be placed in UTC using its receipt timestamp?','No. Keep the source event-time uncertainty; receipt time is a different observation.','A sorting tool cannot create missing timezone/context evidence.'),
   check('Does an alert prove a confirmed incident or human actor?','No. Triage its sources/context and preserve attribution/impact uncertainty.','A detection signal and account/IP labels are not a complete incident verdict.'),
   check('Should response perform unconditional reboot/deletion before considering evidence and harm?','No. The authorised lead balances urgency, operations and appropriate preservation under the plan.','A universal destructive shortcut can remove useful evidence or interrupt necessary work.'),
   check('Does a matching backup digest demonstrate useful recovery within RPO/RTO?','No. Check usable recovery point, access/keys, actual restored data/functions and elapsed recovery time.','Exact bytes are one piece of evidence, not the complete service outcome.'),
   check('What does the six-function framework mapping establish by itself?','Relevant outcome organisation, not a completed test, exhaustive checklist or security certification.','Evidence is needed to demonstrate the mapped outcome.'),
   check('What should determine the next learning stage after this foundation?','Demonstrated/unclear skills, a targeted revision plan and a small authorised goal with evidence criteria.','Completing pages alone is not universal mastery or permission to test external systems.')
  ],
  reflection:'Finish your capstone assessment and sanitised portfolio. Recheck any inconsistent scope/count/time claims, then record two strengths, two gaps, revision steps and one bounded next-stage goal. Keep this final review in your existing day notes.',
  references:[ref('Capstone assessment/readiness workbook','docs/cybersecurity-week8/assessment-workbook.txt'),ref('Worked fictional assessment','docs/cybersecurity-week8/worked-example.txt'),ref('NIST: framework outcomes and limitations','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework/faqs'),ref('NIST: scoped assessment','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/115/final')]
 }
};
